WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.009 --> 00:00:03.619
- When an interactive artwork

00:00:03.619 --> 00:00:07.389
forms an engagement with
people in the audience,

00:00:07.389 --> 00:00:10.406
you can watch a sort of
conversation develop.

00:00:10.406 --> 00:00:12.588
The artwork's not really fully realized

00:00:12.588 --> 00:00:16.755
until those interactions
have been allowed to occur.

00:00:20.014 --> 00:00:22.072
My name's Geoffrey Drake-Brockman,

00:00:22.072 --> 00:00:24.489
and I'm a cybernetics artist.

00:00:25.403 --> 00:00:27.857
I work in the most
isolated city in the world:

00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:30.057
Perth, Western Australia.

00:00:30.057 --> 00:00:33.814
For a long time I had two parallel lives.

00:00:33.814 --> 00:00:36.957
I was an IT guy, a computer programmer,

00:00:36.957 --> 00:00:39.040
and I was also a painter.

00:00:40.943 --> 00:00:42.801
I kept the whole idea of art

00:00:42.801 --> 00:00:45.370
completely separate from computation.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:47.309
And that just sort of led to a crisis.

00:00:47.309 --> 00:00:48.417
I thought, what am I doing?

00:00:48.417 --> 00:00:49.635
Who am I kidding?

00:00:49.635 --> 00:00:50.991
So I let it in,

00:00:50.991 --> 00:00:55.287
and suddenly I was making
artworks which were robotic.

00:00:55.287 --> 00:00:56.942
(soft piano music)

00:00:56.942 --> 00:00:59.731
Cyrernetics is about interaction.

00:00:59.731 --> 00:01:02.172
It can be made out of
any kind of technology.

00:01:02.172 --> 00:01:05.190
You can use electronics,
computers, even clockwork,

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.876
to enable conversations to take place

00:01:07.876 --> 00:01:10.209
between people and artworks.

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:14.758
For me, the whole point
of using technology in art

00:01:14.758 --> 00:01:17.406
is to make an artwork interactive.

00:01:17.406 --> 00:01:20.219
With interactivity, you can
get deep audience engagement.

00:01:20.219 --> 00:01:22.729
People look at a piece for hours on end,

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:24.812
instead of just a minute.

00:01:27.097 --> 00:01:28.311
When I complete an artwork,

00:01:28.311 --> 00:01:30.517
I like to watch it
respond to its audience.

00:01:30.517 --> 00:01:33.060
I watch to see how the
play between the artwork

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.809
and the audience develops.

00:01:34.809 --> 00:01:38.195
Sometimes I saw people who
were treating the artwork

00:01:38.195 --> 00:01:40.064
as though it was a person.

00:01:40.064 --> 00:01:41.501
They were smiling at it,

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.049
waving to it,

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:44.280
talking to it.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.782
I thought, how far can we take this?

00:01:46.782 --> 00:01:48.577
The part that I love most of all

00:01:48.577 --> 00:01:51.870
is when it's done, when
the vision is realized,

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:53.983
when I can set the artwork free,

00:01:53.983 --> 00:01:56.008
and watch it interact with people.

00:01:56.008 --> 00:01:58.612
It's actually doing what it's meant to do,

00:01:58.612 --> 00:02:00.898
and creating what it's meant to create.

00:02:00.898 --> 00:02:04.065
(intense piano music)

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:10.623
(electronic ping)

